## 5. Scaling beyond the 100 MiB limit

If your dataset is larger than what GitHub Pages can store, you have two common patterns:

### 5.1 Multiple regional PMTiles files

```json
{
  "sources": {
    "europe": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "pmtiles://https://example.com/tiles/europe.pmtiles"
    },
    "asia": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "pmtiles://https://example.com/tiles/asia.pmtiles"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    { "id": "water-eu", "type": "fill", "source": "europe", "source-layer": "water", ... },
    { "id": "water-as", "type": "fill", "source": "asia",   "source-layer": "water", ... }
  ]
}
```

Only the source(s) whose bounds intersect the current view will be queried, so the browser only fetches the needed file(s).

### 5.2 Host the archive on a CDN‑friendly bucket

1. Upload `world.pmtiles` to an S3 bucket (or Cloudflare R2).  
2. Set **CORS** (`*`) and **Cache‑Control** (`public, max-age=31536000`).  
3. (Optional) Put CloudFront/Cloudflare in front for global edge caching.  
4. Reference the **HTTPS URL** in the style exactly as shown above – the same `pmtiles://` prefix works for any HTTP URL.

Your GitHub Pages site can still serve the HTML/JS/CSS; the heavy tile file lives elsewhere.

